::JOUR_U05_L02_Q_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.DIG.02] Source: Storyboard: Hallway Recycling Video. Project Goal: Create a 30-second news video showing how students should use the new hallway recycling stations. Sequence: Shot 1 wide shot of the hallway station. Shot 2 close-up of the labels for paper, bottles, and trash. Shot 3 student holds a plastic bottle and pauses before choosing a bin. Shot 4 student places the bottle in the correct bin. Shot 5 final screen says Check the label before you drop it. Sound Plan: Short narration explains each step. Light background sound is allowed only if it does not cover the narration. Accuracy Note: The video must show the real labels and correct bin placement so viewers do not learn the wrong process. Question 1: Why should the storyboard begin with a wide shot? {
~A. The video should begin with a close-up of a random hallway poster.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
=B. The wide shot establishes where the recycling station is before the video shows details.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or presentation skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the choice to the embedded journalism source. This answer works because it supports coherent reporting, accurate visual communication, clear sequence, audience understanding, or credible evidence.
~C. The wide shot should be skipped because setting never matters.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
~D. The video should begin with the ending screen only.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
}
::JOUR_U05_L02_Q_Q02::[MLA.JOUR.MED.08] Source: Storyboard: Hallway Recycling Video. Project Goal: Create a 30-second news video showing how students should use the new hallway recycling stations. Sequence: Shot 1 wide shot of the hallway station. Shot 2 close-up of the labels for paper, bottles, and trash. Shot 3 student holds a plastic bottle and pauses before choosing a bin. Shot 4 student places the bottle in the correct bin. Shot 5 final screen says Check the label before you drop it. Sound Plan: Short narration explains each step. Light background sound is allowed only if it does not cover the narration. Accuracy Note: The video must show the real labels and correct bin placement so viewers do not learn the wrong process. Question 2: Which shot best supports visual accuracy? {
~A. A blurry shot of the bins is enough because viewers can guess the labels.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
~B. Visual accuracy is unnecessary because recycling is familiar.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
~C. The wrong bin placement is acceptable if the video is short.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
=D. The close-up of real labels supports visual accuracy by showing the correct categories.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or presentation skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the choice to the embedded journalism source. This answer works because it supports coherent reporting, accurate visual communication, clear sequence, audience understanding, or credible evidence.
}
::JOUR_U05_L02_Q_Q03::[MLA.JOUR.DIG.03] Source: Storyboard: Hallway Recycling Video. Project Goal: Create a 30-second news video showing how students should use the new hallway recycling stations. Sequence: Shot 1 wide shot of the hallway station. Shot 2 close-up of the labels for paper, bottles, and trash. Shot 3 student holds a plastic bottle and pauses before choosing a bin. Shot 4 student places the bottle in the correct bin. Shot 5 final screen says Check the label before you drop it. Sound Plan: Short narration explains each step. Light background sound is allowed only if it does not cover the narration. Accuracy Note: The video must show the real labels and correct bin placement so viewers do not learn the wrong process. Question 3: Why should narration remain clear? {
=A. Clear narration helps viewers understand each step without confusion.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or presentation skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the choice to the embedded journalism source. This answer works because it supports coherent reporting, accurate visual communication, clear sequence, audience understanding, or credible evidence.
~B. Narration can be covered by music if the music is energetic.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
~C. Narration should include unrelated jokes to keep attention.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
~D. Narration should repeat every source note even if it crowds the video.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
}
::JOUR_U05_L02_Q_Q04::[MLA.JOUR.DIG.02] Source: Storyboard: Hallway Recycling Video. Project Goal: Create a 30-second news video showing how students should use the new hallway recycling stations. Sequence: Shot 1 wide shot of the hallway station. Shot 2 close-up of the labels for paper, bottles, and trash. Shot 3 student holds a plastic bottle and pauses before choosing a bin. Shot 4 student places the bottle in the correct bin. Shot 5 final screen says Check the label before you drop it. Sound Plan: Short narration explains each step. Light background sound is allowed only if it does not cover the narration. Accuracy Note: The video must show the real labels and correct bin placement so viewers do not learn the wrong process. Question 4: Which sequence best teaches the recycling process? {
~A. The best sequence starts with the final reminder before showing the station.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
~B. The best sequence shows the student choosing before viewers see the labels.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
=C. The sequence moves from station location, to labels, to decision, to correct bin placement, to final reminder.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or presentation skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the choice to the embedded journalism source. This answer works because it supports coherent reporting, accurate visual communication, clear sequence, audience understanding, or credible evidence.
~D. The best sequence uses only close-ups with no context.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
}
