~B. The wide shot should be skipped because setting never matters.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
=C. The wide shot establishes where the recycling station is before the video shows details.#Correct. Step 1: identify the production or presentation skill in the standard. Step 2: connect the choice to the embedded journalism source. This answer works because it supports coherent reporting, accurate visual communication, clear sequence, audience understanding, or credible evidence.
~D. The video should begin with the ending screen only.#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source and identify the reporting goal, visual sequence, evidence, or accuracy need. Step 2: compare the option with the lesson model. This option is weak because it distracts from the report, removes needed evidence, creates an unclear sequence, weakens accuracy, or ignores audience understanding.
}
::JOUR_U05_L02_Q_Q17::[MLA.JOUR.MED.08] Source: Storyboard: Hallway Recycling Video. Project Goal: Create a 30-second news video showing how students should use the new hallway recycling stations. Sequence: Shot 1 wide shot of the hallway station. Shot 2 close-up of the labels for paper, bottles, and trash. Shot 3 student holds a plastic bottle and pauses before choosing a bin. Shot 4 student places the bottle in the correct bin. Shot 5 final screen says Check the label before you drop it. Sound Plan: Short narration explains each step. Light background sound is allowed only if it does not cover the narration. Accuracy Note: The video must show the real labels and correct bin placement so viewers do not learn the wrong process. Question 17: Which shot best supports visual accuracy? {
